{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793624224,"nonce":"dHmoW+ymxDDnUlvyzYy6ZXl+GkY=","message":"Loop unswitching on the class test, tried and not kept (2026-11-02, NUC). SOR's inner loop tests G.data's runtime kind (a load of the object's data tag, == 22, loop-invariant) in six diamonds per cell; the joins stop load_cse (it carries loads only down single-predecessor edges), so the Array2D row and length loads and the bounds compares are reloaded per access. A loop_unswitch pass (copy an innermost loop whose invariant condition is used by at least two branches, decide the branches in each copy, merge loop values at the exits; interpreter and Cranelift agreed with a plain reading on its tests) split the outlined resume region SOR_execute$s0$resume1 and lowered the Cranelift tier from 162 to 143 instructions per cell (LLVM 109 to 100). It did not split the main body: the six class tests are six values (per-access loads of the tag) until licm hoists them and load_cse merges them, and by then the pass had had its rounds. Suite A/B (22 kernels, 5 interleaved reps): LLVM ladder score 1.015, wall 1.020, cpu 1.031, scimark_sor score 1.016 (no gain: LLVM unswitches the loop itself and the copy only adds compile time); Cranelift-only ladder score 1.003, wall 0.995, scimark_sor 0.860 score and 0.883 wall, scimark_lu 0.936 and 0.945, the rest noise. It helps a build that ends at Cranelift by about 12% on one kernel and costs the CI configuration compile time, so it was not kept. What CI's SOR loses is the 97 ms before LLVM lands (install 46 ms, opt 30), not Cranelift's loop; the promotion-order knobs did not help over the suite either (earlier comments).","files":null}]}